WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:02.377
Some of Boeing's 737 max.

00:00:02.377 --> 00:00:04.170
Nine planes are grounded right

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.881
now, triggering cancellations nationwide.

00:00:06.881 --> 00:00:08.591
FAA investigators want to make sure

00:00:08.591 --> 00:00:11.594
that no other planes have the same flaw

00:00:11.636 --> 00:00:14.222
potentially as an Alaska Airlines plane

00:00:14.222 --> 00:00:16.182
that made an emergency landing Friday.

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:17.642
A portion of the plane's fuselage

00:00:17.642 --> 00:00:18.685
blew off minutes

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:19.519
after takeoff,

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:21.938
sucking out some passengers belongings.

00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:23.440
Nobody was seriously hurt,

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.150
but the NTSB admitted overnight

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:26.317
that they got lucky.

00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:27.569
CNN's Mike Valerio

00:00:27.569 --> 00:00:28.319
joins us now

00:00:28.319 --> 00:00:30.030
from Portland, Oregon,

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.072
where the plane made

00:00:31.072 --> 00:00:32.240
its emergency landing.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.325
So what have we learned about this

00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:37.579
new investigation?

00:00:37.662 --> 00:00:38.455
Well, Victor and Amber,

00:00:38.455 --> 00:00:39.164
good morning to you.

00:00:39.164 --> 00:00:39.414
You know,

00:00:39.414 --> 00:00:41.416
I think we got a pretty important window

00:00:41.416 --> 00:00:43.334
into the thinking of the NTSB

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:44.794
when they stressed that

00:00:44.794 --> 00:00:46.588
at this point of the investigation,

00:00:46.588 --> 00:00:49.507
they are not, I repeat, not concerned

00:00:49.507 --> 00:00:51.342
with the overall design

00:00:51.342 --> 00:00:54.345
of the wider, larger max nine fleet.

00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:56.931
They're looking at this specific plane.

00:00:56.931 --> 00:00:57.974
And more to the point,

00:00:57.974 --> 00:00:59.726
looking specifically for this

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:02.479
large hunk, the piece of the plane,

00:01:02.479 --> 00:01:03.438
the door plug

00:01:03.438 --> 00:01:05.940
that is still missing, Victor and Amara,

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.443
at this early Sunday morning hour.

00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:10.111
So they narrowed their search

00:01:10.111 --> 00:01:11.571
down, confirming noise yesterday

00:01:11.571 --> 00:01:13.281
night to the Cedar Hills

00:01:13.281 --> 00:01:14.783
community of Greater Portland

00:01:14.783 --> 00:01:16.242
and told our viewers

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:17.285
know around the world

00:01:17.285 --> 00:01:18.703
what we're talking about when we say

00:01:18.703 --> 00:01:20.538
Cedar Hills, We're not describing

00:01:20.538 --> 00:01:21.498
some idyllic,

00:01:21.498 --> 00:01:22.749
uninterrupted section

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:24.501
of beautiful Oregon forest.

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:27.670
This is a suburban, dense community

00:01:27.796 --> 00:01:29.214
close to the airport,

00:01:29.214 --> 00:01:30.840
dozens of subdivisions,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.175
hundreds of homes.

00:01:32.175 --> 00:01:33.593
So the striking visual

00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.261
that we're waiting for today

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:37.180
would be to look for FBI

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.140
agents and NTSB personnel

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:40.558
going door to door,

00:01:40.558 --> 00:01:42.936
looking through people's backyards

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:44.229
for this piece.

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:44.687
The key

00:01:44.687 --> 00:01:47.023
to the investigation of the plane.

00:01:47.023 --> 00:01:49.567
So the NTSB chair, Jennifer Hamadeh,

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:52.445
made it also a specific point to say,

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:53.738
despite this accident,

00:01:53.738 --> 00:01:56.741
how lucky the 171 people on board

00:01:56.866 --> 00:01:58.701
were at the time this happened.

00:01:58.701 --> 00:02:01.704
Listen to her on that front.

00:02:01.746 --> 00:02:02.956
Those watching may be

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:04.082
curious about safety

00:02:04.082 --> 00:02:06.334
or concerned about safety.

00:02:06.334 --> 00:02:09.587
We have the safest

00:02:09.671 --> 00:02:13.299
aviation system in the world.

00:02:13.383 --> 00:02:15.009
It is incredibly safe.

00:02:15.009 --> 00:02:16.970
We are the global gold

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.514
standard for safety around the world.

00:02:19.514 --> 00:02:22.058
But we have to maintain that

00:02:22.058 --> 00:02:28.148
standard.

00:02:28.231 --> 00:02:29.524
And so what's going to happen

00:02:29.524 --> 00:02:31.109
in the early morning hours,

00:02:31.109 --> 00:02:32.861
the flight recorders will be sent

00:02:32.861 --> 00:02:34.654
from Portland to NTSB headquarters

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:35.905
in Washington, D.C..

00:02:35.905 --> 00:02:37.448
John Lovell takes over

00:02:37.448 --> 00:02:39.284
as the chief investigator.

00:02:39.284 --> 00:02:40.243
He has experience.

00:02:40.243 --> 00:02:41.202
Victor Nomura,

00:02:41.202 --> 00:02:43.163
representing the United States,

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:45.039
helping out Ethiopia in authorities

00:02:45.039 --> 00:02:45.999
to investigate

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:48.960
the Max eight crash in 2019.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.211
So he said these cases are

00:02:50.211 --> 00:02:51.379
utterly different.

00:02:51.379 --> 00:02:52.922
But every scene that he works

00:02:52.922 --> 00:02:54.757
on adds to his perspect of,

00:02:54.757 --> 00:02:56.050
as all of us

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:57.218
across the United States and around

00:02:57.218 --> 00:02:58.386
the world are wondering,

00:02:58.386 --> 00:03:00.221
what happened

00:03:00.221 --> 00:03:01.931
to cause this plot emerging. Victor.

00:03:01.931 --> 00:03:03.016
Yeah, exactly.

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:03.725
That is the question.

00:03:03.725 --> 00:03:05.268
Mike Valerio, thank you very much.

00:03:05.268 --> 00:03:06.853
Let's bring in former NTSB

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:08.563
air safety investigator

00:03:08.563 --> 00:03:10.231
Alan Diehl to the conversation.

00:03:10.231 --> 00:03:11.524
He's also the author of Air

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:13.193
Safety Investigators

00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:15.153
Using Science to Save Lives

00:03:15.153 --> 00:03:17.906
One Crash at a Time. Alan.

00:03:17.906 --> 00:03:19.407
It's good to see you this morning.

00:03:19.407 --> 00:03:20.491
First off, you know,

00:03:20.491 --> 00:03:22.410
obviously people who fly

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:24.412
this is very unsettling.

00:03:24.412 --> 00:03:27.332
And while the FAA has grounded

00:03:27.332 --> 00:03:28.499
many of these, Max,

00:03:28.499 --> 00:03:29.500
nine planes

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.668
with these replacement

00:03:30.668 --> 00:03:33.838
plug doors or deactivated doors,

00:03:33.922 --> 00:03:36.925
you know, what will they be looking for

00:03:36.925 --> 00:03:38.218
in terms of safety checks

00:03:38.218 --> 00:03:41.095
to make sure that these planes are

00:03:41.095 --> 00:03:43.973
good to fly?

00:03:44.015 --> 00:03:44.724
Well, they're going to

00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:46.392
have to be very careful.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:48.978
I believe the NTSB chair

00:03:48.978 --> 00:03:50.146
or somebody mentioned

00:03:50.146 --> 00:03:53.107
that this particular airplane

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:54.943
had a pressurization problem.

00:03:54.943 --> 00:03:55.485
It's you know, it's

00:03:55.485 --> 00:03:56.819
only 11 weeks old

00:03:56.819 --> 00:03:58.655
at the time of the accident.

00:03:58.655 --> 00:04:02.200
And if that I'm not suggesting

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.327
that this dark cloud

00:04:04.327 --> 00:04:04.994
was the cause

00:04:04.994 --> 00:04:06.704
of the pressurization problem,

00:04:06.704 --> 00:04:08.039
but they'll be looking at that.

00:04:08.039 --> 00:04:08.623
They'll look at

00:04:08.623 --> 00:04:09.999
they'll interview the mechanics

00:04:09.999 --> 00:04:11.834
that must have opened that door.

00:04:11.834 --> 00:04:13.586
You open it from the outside.

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:14.671
You can open it from

00:04:14.671 --> 00:04:16.506
the inside as a passenger.

00:04:16.506 --> 00:04:18.466
But did they close it properly?

00:04:18.466 --> 00:04:19.425
They'll also be looking

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:21.135
at the manufacturing

00:04:21.135 --> 00:04:22.679
and the quality control

00:04:22.679 --> 00:04:24.180
of this particular product.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.932
So they're going to be busy.

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:26.849
As Mike pointed out,

00:04:26.849 --> 00:04:28.643
they're also going to be obviously

00:04:28.643 --> 00:04:30.311
looking for the missing door

00:04:30.311 --> 00:04:32.146
that may be on somebody's roof.

00:04:32.146 --> 00:04:35.024
Yeah, it's interesting to hear the NTSB

00:04:35.024 --> 00:04:36.693
chair pleading, not pleading, but,

00:04:36.693 --> 00:04:38.319
you know, making the case to the public

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:40.571
to be on the lookout for this panel.

00:04:40.571 --> 00:04:42.282
Let's talk about the fuselage then,

00:04:42.282 --> 00:04:44.284
because you mentioned

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:45.952
they're manufactured

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:51.249
by a subcontractor in Kansas.

00:04:51.332 --> 00:04:54.127
How much oversight is there by Boeing

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:56.045
when when it's fuselages

00:04:56.045 --> 00:05:00.174
are being manufactured on the max nine?

00:05:00.216 --> 00:05:01.968
Well, the company is called

00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:03.094
Spirit Aviation.

00:05:03.094 --> 00:05:05.221
They used to be a division of Boeing,

00:05:05.221 --> 00:05:06.306
but they spun it off for,

00:05:06.306 --> 00:05:07.724
I guess for economic reasons.

00:05:07.724 --> 00:05:10.685
But surely those are

00:05:10.727 --> 00:05:11.394
the Boeing

00:05:11.394 --> 00:05:13.104
people are familiar

00:05:13.104 --> 00:05:14.856
with the assembly procedures

00:05:14.856 --> 00:05:15.481
and the quality

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:16.566
they must have written

00:05:16.566 --> 00:05:18.276
the quality control standards,

00:05:18.276 --> 00:05:20.528
which had to be approved by the FAA.

00:05:20.528 --> 00:05:22.780
Now, you've got to keep in mind

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:23.489
these plug

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:24.615
doors are not

00:05:24.615 --> 00:05:27.618
particularly new to the 737.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:28.328
I understand

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:31.622
they've been out there since 1996.

00:05:31.706 --> 00:05:33.458
And whenever you have a problem

00:05:33.458 --> 00:05:35.001
with a piece of component

00:05:35.001 --> 00:05:36.336
or a piece of equipment

00:05:36.336 --> 00:05:40.214
on an aircraft, you file a maintenance, a

00:05:40.298 --> 00:05:42.675
a maintenance discrepancy report.

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:44.886
So they'll be looking at the history

00:05:44.886 --> 00:05:47.889
of these plug doors also.

00:05:47.889 --> 00:05:48.931
And before we go,

00:05:48.931 --> 00:05:49.599
I got to ask you

00:05:49.599 --> 00:05:52.352
about the history of the 737 max planes,

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:54.937
considering that, you know,

00:05:54.937 --> 00:05:56.522
they've been involved in deadly crashes

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:57.231
and of course,

00:05:57.231 --> 00:05:59.025
in recent weeks and days,

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:00.985
there were production issues

00:06:00.985 --> 00:06:02.570
with these 737 max planes.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:03.404
I mean, is there

00:06:03.404 --> 00:06:06.991
should there be a broader concern

00:06:07.075 --> 00:06:08.910
for some of these Boeing planes,

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:13.247
specifically the 737 max line?

00:06:13.331 --> 00:06:14.624
Well, I.

00:06:14.624 --> 00:06:16.334
Boeing has a long history

00:06:16.334 --> 00:06:17.794
of engineering excellence,

00:06:17.794 --> 00:06:19.837
and other people have pointed out

00:06:19.837 --> 00:06:21.923
that they became

00:06:21.923 --> 00:06:24.926
more commercial early again

00:06:25.009 --> 00:06:26.969
in recent decades.

00:06:26.969 --> 00:06:28.888
But because they only have one

00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:30.014
competitor, Airbus.

00:06:30.014 --> 00:06:33.101
And right now, Airbus is doing very well.

00:06:33.142 --> 00:06:36.145
So the short answer is no.

00:06:36.187 --> 00:06:39.107
But I do think their

00:06:39.190 --> 00:06:40.400
continued scrutiny

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.193
needs to be maintained.

00:06:42.193 --> 00:06:44.404
And we can talk about the relationship

00:06:44.404 --> 00:06:45.113
of the FAA.

00:06:45.113 --> 00:06:47.615
I work for the FAA as a scientist,

00:06:47.615 --> 00:06:49.242
as you may have mentioned,

00:06:49.242 --> 00:06:51.369
as well as an NTSB investigator.

00:06:51.369 --> 00:06:53.246
But the up there,

00:06:53.246 --> 00:06:54.997
the Boeing needs to be watched

00:06:54.997 --> 00:06:56.833
and the FAA needs the resources

00:06:56.833 --> 00:06:58.209
to do that.

00:06:58.209 --> 00:06:59.377
Alan Dale, we'll leave it there.

00:06:59.377 --> 00:07:00.253
Thank you very much.

